Why your Terraform state file must never stay local

Think of the state file as your project’s ledger. If it only lives on your laptop:

→ Sensitive data is exposed
→ One delete/crash = lost infra truth
→ Team collaboration breaks instantly

✅ Remote backend = safety + scale

Store state in a central, secure location:

→ AWS S3 (most common)
→ Azure Blob Storage
→ GCP Cloud Storage

Every terraform plan or apply fetches state remotely, compares infra, and writes updates back — no fragile local folders.

💡 Local = single point of failure
Remote = shared truth for the team

🔧 Example: S3 backend config

terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket  = "my-tf-state"
    key     = "prod/terraform.tfstate"
    region  = "us-east-1"
    encrypt = true
  }
}
